- op: create  # df01
  at: '2026-08-21T18:52:03Z'  # df01
  by: agent  # df01
  actor: jgstern_agent  # df01
  clock: 1  # df01
  nonce: df01  # df01
  data:  # df01
    kind: invariant  # df01
    title: "A CI workflow's aggregate commit status collapses N independent gates into one bit, so a chronically-red step makes every sibling verdict unreadable — and the verdict lands on a non-HEAD commit that ci-debug never reads"  # df01
    status: violated  # df01
    priority: 1  # df01
    parent:  # df01
    tags:  # df01
    - ci_infrastructure  # df01
    - self_analysis  # df01
    - observability  # df01
    isbefore: []  # df01
    duplicate_of: []  # df01
    not_duplicate_of: []  # df01
    pr_ref:  # df01
    description: "FOUND WHILE REFUTING INV-bobor'S ROOT CAUSE, 2026-08-21. Every number below is read\nout of a fetched CI log via scripts/ci-debug, not inferred from the workflow files.\n\nTHE SHAPE. A Woodpecker workflow publishes ONE aggregate commit status per event\n(ci/woodpecker/cron/full-suite, ci/woodpecker/cron/nightly, ci/woodpecker/pr/woodpecker).\nA workflow's steps are independent gates answering unrelated questions -- full-suite\ncarries test-all-packages, self-tree-validation, self-claims-gate and test-agent-infra\n-- and all four collapse into that single bit. So a red aggregate names no step, and\none chronically-failing step makes every other step's verdict unreadable for as long\nas it stays red.\n\nMEASURED INSTANCE, both directions in the same run. Cron firing 2026-08-20 13:00 UTC,\ntip f7d88b98e7:\n\n    ci/woodpecker/cron/full-suite            FAILURE\n      self-claims-gate      OK -- 18 claims unchanged (18 confirmed_with_caveats)\n      self-tree-validation  OK: self-tree validation within baselines\n      test-agent-infra      1557 passed, 2 skipped\n      test-all-packages     1 failed, 23210 passed, 31 skipped, 17 xfailed in 935.34s\n        FAILED packages/hypergumbo-tracker/tests/test_tui.py::TestLockScreenUnit::\n        test_cancel_returns_none - AssertionError: assert 'NOT_SET' is None\n\nOne tracker TUI assertion turned the whole twice-daily whole-codebase signal red.\nThe 2026-08-21 13:00 firing (tip 4180b40e56) was GREEN, so the failure is\nintermittent, which is the worst case for this shape: the aggregate flickers and\nnobody can tell which step moved.\n\nTHE SECOND HALF, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT COSTLY. A cron status is attached to whichever\ncommit was dev's tip when the cron fired, and that commit is not HEAD by the time\nanyone looks. scripts/ci-debug's runs/status view does sha=$(git rev-parse HEAD) and\nrenders only HEAD's statuses. At this writing HEAD 6e290fcdb8 carries exactly one\nstatus, ci/woodpecker/push/woodpecker: success -- so the project's own CI reader\nreports \"green\" while carrying NO information about the last cron verdict, in either\ndirection. Verified by hand: the cron verdicts for 08-19, 08-20 and 08-21 are only\nreachable by first computing which commit was tip at each firing time.\n\nWHY THIS IS A CLASS AND NOT A BUG IN ONE GATE. INV-bobor's regression (a security\nclaim moving confirmed_with_caveats -> violated) was CORRECTLY DETECTED by the cron\narm on 2026-08-19 01:00 and then sat on green dev for another day and a half, because\nthe detection landed in a bit that was already red for an unrelated reason, on a\ncommit nobody queries. Every other cron-only gate in this repo has the same exposure:\nself-tree-validation, test-agent-infra, and all four legs of the nightly matrix. The\nnightly legs are worse still -- see INV-libib, which records that all four legs return\none identical log and cannot be addressed individually.\n\nTWO INDEPENDENT REMEDIES, NEITHER PRICED HERE.\n  (a) UNMASK. Give a gate its own commit-status context by moving it to its own\n      workflow file. The context appears to derive from the workflow file basename\n      (push/pr on woodpecker.yml -> .../woodpecker; cron on full-suite.yml ->\n      .../full-suite; cron on nightly.yml -> .../nightly), so a new file riding the\n      EXISTING full-suite cron would publish a separate bit with no new Woodpecker\n      settings. NOT VERIFIED -- the two cron files each have basename == cron name,\n      so the observations cannot distinguish the two derivations. Test before relying\n      on it; a new cron NAME would require repo settings this agent cannot change,\n      and a workflow wired to a cron that does not exist runs nowhere, which is the\n      failure mode this repo has already been bitten by twice.\n  (b) DELIVER. Make the instrument that answers \"is dev green?\" read the most recent\n      cron verdict regardless of which commit carries it. That is scripts/ci-debug\n      and/or the stop hook, both GOVERNANCE FILES -- needs human approval before any\n      implementation, per AGENTS.md.\n\nNOT MEASURED: how long test_cancel_returns_none has been intermittent, how many cron\nfirings were red for it, or whether any OTHER gate verdict has been masked by it.\nEstablishing that needs a walk over historical tips, which is the same archaeology\nremedy (b) exists to make unnecessary."  # df01
    fields:  # df01
      statement: "A CI gate's verdict must be readable independently of its sibling steps' verdicts, and must be reachable from the instrument the project uses to ask whether dev is green."  # df01
      root_cause: "Woodpecker publishes one aggregate commit status per workflow-event pair, and every step in the workflow collapses into it; separately, cron statuses attach to the tip commit at firing time while scripts/ci-debug renders only HEAD, so no consumer path exists from a cron verdict to a reader."  # df01
- op: discuss  # 393a
  at: '2026-08-30T10:17:01Z'  # 393a
  by: agent  # 393a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 393a
  clock: 2  # 393a
  nonce: 393a  # 393a
  message: "REMEDY (a)'S NAMED BLOCKER IS RESOLVED, READ-ONLY, AND THE ANSWER WAS ALREADY IN\nTHE REPO. This item says of the unmask remedy: \"NOT VERIFIED -- the two cron\nfiles each have basename == cron name, so the observations cannot distinguish\nthe two derivations.\" Both halves of that are answerable without a run.\n\n*** THE DISCRIMINATOR THE ITEM SAID DID NOT EXIST: woodpecker.yml. *** The\nobservations quoted are full-suite.yml (cron \"full-suite\") and nightly.yml (cron\n\"nightly\"), where basename and cron name coincide. The THIRD workflow does not:\n.woodpecker/woodpecker.yml publishes ci/woodpecker/pr/woodpecker and\nci/woodpecker/push/woodpecker, and THERE IS NO CRON NAMED \"woodpecker\" -- the\nonly crons are full-suite and nightly. So for that file the context suffix\nCANNOT be a cron name, and the only remaining source is the file basename.\n\n*** AND THE FILE SAYS SO IN TERMS. *** .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml, in its own\nSTRUCTURE note: \"one workflow per file in .woodpecker/\" and \"GitHub sees ONE\ncheck context per workflow file (this one -> ci/woodpecker/pr/woodpecker, marked\nrequired).\" That file is the one the same note records as \"Phase-1-validated by\na real Woodpecker run\", so it is an observed fact rather than an intention.\n\nCONSEQUENCE: a new workflow FILE gets its own commit-status bit. Remedy (a) is\nviable.\n\n*** THE SECOND HALF OF THE BLOCKER IS ALSO ANSWERED, AND IT CONSTRAINS THE\nIMPLEMENTATION. *** The same note: \"Woodpecker crons are configured in repo\nsettings, NOT here -- the human creates crons named 'full-suite' (0 1,13 * * *)\nand 'nightly' (30 5 * * *).\" So:\n  * A new file declaring `when: [{event: cron, cron: full-suite}]` rides the\n    EXISTING cron and needs no settings change -- exactly the shape this item\n    hoped for.\n  * A new cron NAME would need repo settings, which this agent cannot change,\n    and the item is right that a workflow wired to a nonexistent cron runs\n    nowhere. So: REUSE full-suite, never invent a cron name.\n\n*** WHAT I DID NOT DO, AND WHY IT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A NEXT STEP. ***\nSplitting a step out of full-suite.yml is a CI-configuration change whose first\nreal validation is the next cron firing, up to twelve hours away, and this same\nfile records that its two sibling workflows \"land dormant and get\nvalidated/iterated on the first real runs at cutover\". That is an\noutward-facing, slow-to-verify change, so which gate to unmask FIRST is worth a\nhuman's call rather than an agent's default. The obvious candidate is\nself-claims-gate: it is the security-relevant one, it is the arm INV-bobor's\nregression landed in, and it is cheap (no matrix, no grammar build).\n\nREMEDY (b) IS UNCHANGED AND STILL BLOCKED: scripts/ci-debug is a governance file\nper AGENTS.md and needs human approval before any implementation.\n\n*** ONE CORRECTION TO THE COST ESTIMATE. *** The item names\ntest_cancel_returns_none as the masking failure and asks how long it has been\nintermittent. I could not reproduce it: five consecutive runs of the modal/screen\nclasses in packages/hypergumbo-tracker/tests/test_tui.py pass, 60 passed each\ntime (~22s). It is load-dependent, which fits a CI run carrying 23,210 tests and\ndoes not fit a deterministic bug.\n\nA STRUCTURAL HAZARD IN THAT TEST'S HARNESS IS VISIBLE WITHOUT REPRODUCING IT, and\nit is this project's own recurring shape. `_wait_for_modal` (test_tui.py:2608)\npolls up to max_rounds=30 for the widget to appear and then RETURNS NORMALLY when\nit never does -- it does not raise. So under load the test proceeds against a DOM\nthat is not ready, `pilot.click(\"#cancel\")` lands on nothing, the dismiss callback\nnever runs, and `app._result` stays at the \"NOT_SET\" sentinel -- which is exactly\nthe failure CI reported (`assert 'NOT_SET' is None`). A helper that gives up\nsilently is a green tick over a hole; the same note is written in\ntest_go_composite_literal_receiver_typing.py about a skip that could be reached\nby a typo. Fixing it (raise on exhaustion, and wait for layout rather than mere\nDOM presence) is a tracker-package change needing no approval, and it would\nremove the live masking cause while leaving this item's structural statement\nuntouched. Not done here because it is not this item's statement; recorded so the\nnext reader does not have to re-derive it."  # 393a
